# CASM WP40 trusted reference: casmrelop2
#
# Independently hand-assembled from the casmrelop2 fixture source in
# cmake/GenerateCasmTestFixtures.cmake. NOT produced by CASM. Proves the
# two-sided extraction cases found during WP40's own research (plan
# Dependency Review items 1-2): LDA >TARGET (absolute mode, promoted there
# by FORCE_ABS rather than immediate, since a bare `>TARGET` operand is not
# `#`-prefixed) and .WORD >TARGET both put the real relocatable byte in the
# ValLo position with ValHi as applyExtraction's zero pad -- the opposite
# of casmrelop1's normal case. Cross-check:
#   - brain/plans/2026-07-24-casm-phase8-wp40-relocation-table-and-emission-hooks.md
#
# Source (no .ORG):
#   - START: LDA >TARGET
#   - .WORD >TARGET
#   - TARGET: NOP
#
# START = $3400 (no bytes). LDA >TARGET is 3 bytes ($3400-$3402, absolute
# mode opcode $AD -- FORCE_ABS is set for any symbol-derived operand
# regardless of extraction, so this is never immediate/zero-page): ValLo =
# TARGET's high byte, ValHi = $00 (applyExtraction's pad). .WORD >TARGET is
# 2 bytes ($3403-$3404, same ValLo/ValHi shape). TARGET = $3405
# (immediately after), where NOP ($EA) is emitted.
#
# Hand assembly:
#   00 34        PRG load-address header ($3400, little-endian)
#   AD 34 00     LDA $0034                    (TARGET=$3405's high byte in
#                                              ValLo=$34; ValHi=$00 pad)
#   34 00        .WORD $0034                  (same shape: $34, $00)
#   EA           NOP                          (at $3405, defines TARGET)
#
# bytes: 8
# sha256: 5e0a399dde69f3ed9709459f9359b53688c64a2f8960f2e5134df14e6702d3da

00 34
AD 34 00
34 00
EA
